#          either: it replaces veil/003-audio-fingerprint.patch, which is dropped from
#          patches/series in the same change. Hence the 950-999 range rather than 903 -- see
#          patches/veil/README.md -> "Veil-authored patches".
# Source:  n/a -- authored in this repository.
# Commit:  n/a -- see this repository's git log for this file.
# License: BSD-3-Clause (see CREDITS.md), same terms as the code it edits.
# Changes: n/a -- not a port.
#
# Perturbs the RENDERED SAMPLES of an OfflineAudioContext, deterministically from
# --fingerprint=<seed>, instead of perturbing the reported sample rate. Gated off by
# --disable-spoofing=audio, the same key 003 read.
#
# Why 003 is replaced rather than corrected. Measured, Veil/docs/MIGRATION-UNGOOGLED.md 5.1f and 5.1u:
#
# 1. It reported a sample rate the page did not ask for. 003 added noise in +/-0.01 Hz to the
#    OfflineAudioContext's sample rate, so a page constructing OfflineAudioContext(1, N, 44100) got
#    back 44100.0078125 (seed 7) or 44100.00390625 (seed 8). No real browser can do that -- the
#    constructor's sampleRate argument IS what the context reports -- and Number.isInteger() on it is
#    a one-expression detector needing no knowledge of the seed. The live AudioContext in the same
#    page still reported exactly 44100, so the two contradicted each other. It fired for roughly four
#    seeds in five, which is worse than the marker 007 was dropped for.
#
# 2. For the other seed in five it did nothing at all. The noise was added to a FLOAT sample rate,
#    and a float's ULP at 44100 is 2^-8 = 0.00390625, so any |noise| below half an ULP --
#    0.001953125, i.e. 19.5% of the +/-0.01 range -- rounded away and left the rate bit-identical to
#    unpatched. What survived was quantised to multiples of 0.00390625, so the whole surface carried
#    about five distinct values rather than one per seed.
#
# 3. So the two fixes 5.1f named were both wrong. Scaling the noise to at least one ULP fixes (2) by
#    making (1) larger and more visible; perturbing the samples is the only place the noise can go.
#
# 4. Its getNoiseData() helper was dead. Defined, never called -- which is why buffer.length measured
#    exactly 44100*3 = 132300 in every snapshot at every seed.
#
# The perturbation is relative (samples[i] *= 1 + noise, noise uniform in +/-1e-4) rather than
# additive, so it has no dead zone: the relative ULP of a float is 2^-24 ~= 6e-8, so a sample fails
# to move only for about 0.06% of draws, and a buffer of 132300 frames failing entirely has
# probability ~0. -80 dB is inaudible and well inside the spread real DSP rounding produces. Silent
# channels are skipped: a multiplicative factor leaves 0 at 0, and skipping also avoids clearing
# AudioChannel's silent flag.
#
# It is keyed on the seed, the channel index and the ABSOLUTE frame index, so it does not depend on
# render quantum boundaries or on a suspend/resume, and it is identical in every realm and every run.
# The hook is the point in DoOfflineRendering() where a quantum has been rendered and is about to be
# copied into the shared render target, so it runs exactly once over each frame and never on the
# allocation-failure path.
#
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webaudio/offline_audio_destination_handler.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webaudio/offline_audio_destination_handler.cc
